// doc is the bytes of builtin.tck.
pub var doc = "// Package builtin documents the types, values and functions Tack provides\n// without a declaration or an import. The compiler implements every one of\n// them itself; this file is built into the compiler and is never compiled\n// as code. It exists so editors can show each builtin's documentation and\n// go to its declaration. Changing a copy of it changes nothing.\n//\n// Some builtins take any list, map, arena, pool or type, which no Tack\n// declaration can say exactly. Their declarations below use stand-in types, Elem, Key,\n// Sized and ListType, declared at the end of this file for documentation\n// only; no program can name them.\npackage builtin\n\n// Types\n\n// int64 is a signed 64-bit integer, from -9223372036854775808 to\n// 9223372036854775807. An integer constant with nothing to give it a type\n// is an int64.\ntype int64 int64\n\n// int32 is a signed 32-bit integer, from -2147483648 to 2147483647.\ntype int32 int32\n\n// int16 is a signed 16-bit integer, from -32768 to 32767.\ntype int16 int16\n\n// int8 is a signed 8-bit integer, from -128 to 127.\ntype int8 int8\n\n// uint64 is an unsigned 64-bit integer, from 0 to 18446744073709551615.\ntype uint64 uint64\n\n// uint32 is an unsigned 32-bit integer, from 0 to 4294967295.\ntype uint32 uint32\n\n// uint16 is an unsigned 16-bit integer, from 0 to 65535.\ntype uint16 uint16\n\n// uint8 is an unsigned 8-bit integer, from 0 to 255: a byte.\ntype uint8 uint8\n\n// unsafe_size_t is C's size_t: an unsigned integer as wide as the target\n// makes it, from 32 to 64 bits. Its rules hold whatever its width is.\ntype unsafe_size_t unsafe_size_t\n\n// unsafe_uintptr_t is C's uintptr_t: an unsigned integer wide enough to\n// hold an address, from 32 to 64 bits, not necessarily as wide as\n// unsafe_size_t.\ntype unsafe_uintptr_t unsafe_uintptr_t\n\n// float64 is an IEEE 754 binary64 float, with + - * and / and the six\n// comparisons. A float constant with nothing to give it a type is a\n// float64. Dividing by zero gives an infinity, or NaN for 0.0 / 0.0, and\n// NaN is equal to nothing, itself included. A float carries no facts,\n// cannot index, and is not converted to or from an integer implicitly.\ntype float64 float64\n\n// float32 is an IEEE 754 binary32 float, as float64 is a binary64 one.\ntype float32 float32\n\n// bool is true or false.\ntype bool bool\n\n// string is an immutable string of bytes. Its length is len(s), and s[i]\n// is its byte i, a uint8.\ntype string string\n\n// any refers to a value of one of the basic types, which one known only\n// at run time. It can only be the type of a parameter, or the element\n// type of a variadic or view parameter.\ntype any any\n\n// error is a returned error: a code and a message, code 0 meaning no\n// error, whatever the message. A function that can fail returns one as its\n// last result, and the results beside it cannot be read until it is\n// checked.\ntype error struct {\n    code int32\n    msg  string\n}\n\n// Allocator is an opaque handle on an allocator, which a list grows and is\n// freed through. It is copyable, and has no zero value, no order and no ==.\n// A region's allocator, the name of region r { ... }, is one too, but\n// second class: it is only given to make, passed to a parameter written\n// scope a Allocator, or captured by a closure, and such a parameter is\n// under the same rule in its function. Any Allocator may be passed to a\n// scope one. An extern func cannot take one as scope, so a region's\n// allocator never reaches C.\ntype Allocator Allocator\n\n// id is written id[T]: an index into an arena[T], naming one of its\n// elements, given by add. It is 32 bits, copyable, has == and != and may\n// be a map's key, but has no order and no arithmetic, and converts to and\n// from no integer. Its zero value is the sentinel's id, 0. An arena reads\n// and writes the element an id names with no proof: an id at or past the\n// arena's length, which may come from another arena, names its sentinel.\ntype id id\n\n// span is written span[T]: a run of consecutive elements of an arena[T],\n// its start and its length, each 32 bits, given by add_all. It is\n// copyable, has == and != and may be a map's key, and its zero value is\n// the empty span. a[s] is a read-only view of the span's elements, empty\n// when the span is not wholly in a.\ntype span span\n\n// handle is written handle[T]: one element of a pool[T], given by insert:\n// its slot, the slot's generation and the pool's tag, 32 bits each. It\n// is copyable, has == and != and may be a map's key, but has no order and\n// no arithmetic, and converts to and from no integer. Its zero value is a\n// handle no pool gives. Removing the element moves its slot's generation\n// on, and a slot whose generation would wrap is never used again, so a\n// handle never names another element; nor does one from another pool,\n// whose tag differs, except that past 2^32 - 2 pools made, later pools\n// share the last tag and may accept each other's handles, a logic limit,\n// never a read of memory a pool does not hold. has(p, h) proves it live,\n// and so does a range over p, for its handle, in its body.\ntype handle handle\n\n// Constraints\n\n// Data constrains a type parameter to plain data, [T Data]: a bool, an\n// integer or a float type, text.Text, a list[E] whose E is Data, a\n// map[text.Text]V whose V is Data, or a struct whose every field is Data,\n// private ones included, so a type is Data or not whichever package asks.\n// Nothing else is: no\n// string, which is only ever a literal, no union, array, view, any, id,\n// span, handle, arena, pool, function value or Allocator, and no type\n// parameter constrained by an interface. A call that binds T to a type\n// that is not Data is an error naming the field, element or value type\n// deepest in it that is not. A value of a type parameter constrained by\n// Data has no method, no field and no ==, and is borrowed, moved and freed\n// as any type parameter's value is; a fields loop walks its fields (see\n// fields), and switch T { case int64: ... } runs, in each instance, the\n// one case its type argument matches, in which a case naming one type\n// makes every value of type T a value of that type. Data is closed, so a\n// switch on T with no default must name every kind of type T may stand\n// for, and make(T, alloc) gives any Data type's empty value (see make).\n// Data is a builtin name, which no program can declare.\ntype Data interface {\n}\n\n// Values\n\n// true is the bool that holds.\nvar true bool\n\n// false is the bool that does not hold.\nvar false bool\n\n// noerror is the error with code 0 and an empty message: no error.\nvar noerror error\n\n// c_allocator is the Allocator that lowers to C's malloc, realloc and\n// free. The only other allocators are regions', whose chunks it gives.\nvar c_allocator Allocator\n\n// Functions\n\n// print writes s to standard output exactly as given, adding no newline.\n// It reports no error: a closed descriptor or a failed write loses the\n// bytes. A write to a pipe with no reader, which fails with EPIPE since\n// the program ignores SIGPIPE, ends the process as exit(141) does: every\n// allocator frees every buffer it still holds, no defer runs, and the\n// status is 141, 128 plus SIGPIPE's 13. Standard output is buffered, so\n// the broken pipe may only be found by a later write, by exit, or by the\n// flush once main returns, which end the process the same way.\nfunc print(s string)\n\n// exit ends the process immediately with the given status. Pending defers\n// do not run, in the calling function or any other, but before the\n// process ends every allocator frees every buffer it still holds, so no\n// list outlives the program. It flushes standard output first, and when\n// that finds a pipe with no reader the status is 141, as print's broken\n// pipe ends the process, whatever code was. Only the low bits of the\n// status reach the operating system, the low 8 on Linux and macOS, so\n// exit(70000) ends with status 112.\nfunc exit(code int32)\n\n// len returns the number of elements of the array, view, list or arena v,\n// an arena's sentinel among them, the number of live elements of the pool\n// v, the number of keys of the map v, or the number of bytes of the string\n// v. The length of an array, and of a string literal, is a constant; a\n// view's, a list's, an arena's or a pool's is never known.\nfunc len(v Sized) int64\n\n// fields(T) is what a fields loop ranges over, its only use: T is a type\n// parameter constrained by Data, or a struct type that is Data, and\n//\n//     for f := range fields(T) {\n//         ...\n//     }\n//\n// runs its body once for each field of the type T stands for whose own\n// mark lets the package declaring the function holding the loop see it,\n// in declaration order: every field in the struct's own package, a pub\n// field anywhere, a lib field within its module, whatever the struct's\n// visibility; and not at all for a type with none, a basic type, a list,\n// a map or text.Text outside std/text. f is no value: it cannot be stored,\n// passed or returned, only used as f.name, the field's name, a string, as\n// f.index, the field's position among those the loop runs for, from 0, an\n// int64, and as v.[f], the field f of v, a value of type T. v.[f] is a\n// place, read, assigned and passed as mut as any field of v is, and a\n// field cannot be moved on its own; its type is a type parameter of the\n// loop's own, constrained by Data, which v.[f] of every v of type T\n// shares. The body is checked once, as a loop body that may run any\n// number of times, so what it may do does not depend on the fields, and\n// it may not break or continue the loop; return, ? and defer work in it\n// as in any loop body, a defer running at the end of each field's pass.\nfunc fields(t StructType)\n\n// make returns an empty list of elements of type T, written make(list[T],\n// alloc), or an empty map, make(map[K]V, alloc), which grows through alloc\n// and is freed through it when its owner's block ends. make(arena[T],\n// sentinel, alloc) returns an arena holding only sentinel, which it moves\n// in, as its element at id 0, and make(pool[T], alloc) an empty pool with\n// a tag of its own. make(T, alloc), T a Data type written by its name,\n// returns T's empty value: 0, 0.0 or false for a basic type, an empty\n// text.Text, list or map growing through alloc, and for a struct every\n// field made the same way, private ones included. T may be a type\n// parameter constrained by Data, a case's narrowed or declared one\n// among them, and the make is checked once, against Data. Each allocates\n// nothing, so it cannot fail. alloc may be a region's allocator, or a\n// scope Allocator parameter.\nfunc make(t ListType, alloc Allocator) ListType\n\n// append appends elems to the end of l, in order, growing its buffer\n// through l's allocator when it has no room for them, and returns no\n// error; or, when the allocator cannot grow it, or l's length would\n// overflow an int64, returns error{code: 1, msg: \"out of memory\"} and\n// leaves l as it was, all or nothing. It takes any number of values, none\n// included, append(mut l, a, b, c), or a view spread whole, append(mut l,\n// v...), never a view of l itself. Its error cannot be discarded.\nfunc append[T Elem](mut l list[T], elems ...T) error\n\n// reserve makes room in l for n more elements, so that its capacity is at\n// least its length plus n, and returns no error; its length and elements\n// never change, and n of 0 does nothing. A negative n returns\n// error{code: 2, msg: \"negative count\"}, and one the allocator cannot make\n// room for error{code: 1, msg: \"out of memory\"}, each leaving l as it was.\n// l may be an arena, reserve(mut a, n), which then fails too when a could\n// not hold n more without passing 2^32 - 1 elements. Its error cannot be\n// discarded.\nfunc reserve[T Elem](mut l list[T], n int64) error\n\n// pop moves the last element of l out of it and returns it, leaving l one\n// element shorter; nothing is copied, allocated or freed. Its plain form,\n// x := pop(mut l), needs len(l) > 0 proven where it is written. Its\n// checked form, x, ok := pop(mut l), may be written anywhere: ok is false\n// for an empty list, which is left as it was. Written as a statement,\n// pop(mut l) discards the last element when there is one.\nfunc pop[T Elem](mut l list[T]) T\n\n// remove moves element i of l out of it and returns it, moving every\n// element after it down one place, so the rest keep their order. Its plain\n// form needs i proven in range for l; its checked form, x, ok :=\n// remove(mut l, i), may be written anywhere, ok being false for an i out of\n// range. Written as a statement it discards element i only when i is in range.\n// l may be a pool, remove(mut p, h), which moves out the element the\n// handle h names: v, ok := remove(mut p, h) gives it and true, or, when h\n// names no live element of p, the element type's zero value and false,\n// leaving p as it was; written as a statement, remove(mut p, h) frees the\n// element when there is one. Neither needs a proof, and a pool's remove\n// has no plain form.\nfunc remove[T Elem](mut l list[T], i int64) T\n\n// swap swaps elements i and j of l in place; i equal to j leaves l as it\n// was. It moves, copies and frees nothing, so it reorders lists of owned\n// elements. Both indexes need proofs, as l[i] does, and a proof about\n// len(l) holds across it.\nfunc swap[T Elem](mut l list[T], i int64, j int64)\n\n// replace moves the value of place out, as its result, and moves new into\n// place, in one step, so the place never holds no value: old :=\n// replace(mut s.items, make(list[T], alloc)) takes a list out of a field,\n// which no move can do. place is anything a mut argument can be. Written\n// as a statement, replace(mut place, new) is an assignment, which frees the\n// old value.\nfunc replace[T Elem](mut place T, new T) T\n\n// put puts v under k in m: in place of the value k has, which it frees,\n// keeping k's position in m's order; or at the end of the order when k is\n// new. When the allocator cannot grow m it returns error{code: 1, msg:\n// \"out of memory\"}, leaves m exactly as it was, and frees k and v. k and v\n// are moved in. Its error cannot be discarded.\nfunc put[K Key, V Elem](mut m map[K]V, k K, v V) error\n\n// delete takes the value under k out of m, moving it, and frees the key m\n// held: v, ok := delete(mut m, k) gives the value and true, or, when k is\n// not in m, V's zero value and false. Written as a statement, delete(mut\n// m, k) frees the value it takes.\nfunc delete[K Key, V Elem](mut m map[K]V, k K) (V, bool)\n\n// has reports whether k is in m. Tested as a condition it proves k is in m\n// where the condition holds, so m[k] is then a place that can be read and\n// assigned in place. The proof ends at any write to m that can reach the\n// use. m may be a pool, has(p, h), which reports whether the handle h\n// names a live element of p and, tested as a condition, proves it, so\n// p[h] is a place in the same way, until any insert, remove, assignment\n// or mut use of p that can reach the use.\nfunc has[K Key, V Elem](m map[K]V, k K) bool\n\n// drop frees x at once, exactly as the end of its block would, and leaves\n// it moved from: a later use is a use after a move, until x is assigned a\n// new value, and the end of its block frees nothing more. x is a local\n// variable or an own parameter whose type owns something, written own x;\n// a field or an element cannot be dropped, since it cannot be moved out\n// of what holds it, and a value that owns nothing is an error, since there\n// is nothing to free. In a generic function, drop(own t) of a type\n// parameter's value is allowed, and drops nothing for a type argument\n// that owns nothing. It is only a statement; it has no value.\nfunc drop[T Elem](own x T)\n\n// add moves v into a as its new last element and returns its id, which\n// names it for as long as a lives, and no error; or, when the allocator\n// cannot grow a, or a would hold more than 2^32 - 1 elements, its\n// sentinel among them, the zero id and error{code: 1, msg: \"out of\n// memory\"}, leaving a as it was and freeing v. The id cannot be read\n// until the error is checked, and the error cannot be discarded.\nfunc add[T Elem](mut a arena[T], v T) (id[T], error)\n\n// add_all moves elems into a, in order, as its new last elements, all or\n// none, and returns their span and no error; or, as add fails, the empty\n// span and error{code: 1, msg: \"out of memory\"}, leaving a as it was and\n// freeing what it was given. It takes any number of values, none\n// included, which give the empty span, or a view spread whole,\n// add_all(mut a, v...), whose elements are copied, so not one of an owned\n// element type, and never a view of a itself. The span cannot be read\n// until the error is checked, and the error cannot be discarded.\nfunc add_all[T Elem](mut a arena[T], elems ...T) (span[T], error)\n\n// insert moves v into p and returns its handle, which names it until it is\n// removed, and no error, taking a slot an earlier remove freed when there\n// is one, and otherwise growing p through its allocator; or, when it\n// cannot grow p, or p would hold more than 2^32 - 1 slots, the zero handle\n// and error{code: 1, msg: \"out of memory\"}, leaving p as it was and\n// freeing v. The handle cannot be read until the error is checked, and\n// the error cannot be discarded.\nfunc insert[T Elem](mut p pool[T], v T) (handle[T], error)\n\n// Stand-ins, for documentation only\n\n// Elem stands for any type: a list's, an arena's or a pool's element, a\n// map's value, a place.\ntype Elem interface {\n}\n\n// Key stands for any type a map key can have: one with ==.\ntype Key interface {\n}\n\n// Sized stands for any array, view, list, arena or pool, of any element\n// type, a map, or a string.\ntype Sized interface {\n}\n\n// ListType stands for a list, a map, an arena or a pool type written where\n// a value goes, as make(list[int64], c_allocator) does.\ntype ListType interface {\n}\n\n// StructType stands for a struct type, or a type parameter constrained by\n// Data, written where a value goes, as fields(T) does.\ntype StructType interface {\n}\n"

// header is the bytes of tack.h.
pub var header = "// The C every Tack program shares: the string type and the macros that\n// build strings, the error type and the macros that build errors,\n// any, its type ids and the helpers that read one, and the allocator\n// handle and the macros that read and grow a list, what an arena's add\n// gives and its limit, a pool's handle, what its insert gives, its tags,\n// its slots' first generation and its limit, and the abort an\n// unreachable statement lowers to,\n// defined here once for the generated source, the C files of the standard\n// library and a program's own C files alike. Each\n// includes it as <tack/tack.h>, and the driver compiles each with this\n// file's include directory, $TACK_HOME/src/std/include, on the include\n// path.\n//\n// It holds only what is the same for every program. The tack.h a build\n// generates for a program's own C files is another file: it declares that\n// program's extern funcs and the types their signatures use, and includes\n// this one when a signature uses a string, an error, any, a list or an\n// Allocator. The generated source always includes it.\n//\n// Every name it defines, its include guard among them, is reserved: no\n// extern func or type a program's tack.h declares may take one.\n\n#ifndef TACK_STD_TACK_H\n#define TACK_STD_TACK_H\n\n#include <stdbool.h>\n#include <stddef.h>\n#include <stdint.h>\n\n// A Tack string, passed by value: a pointer to its bytes and their count.\n// Its bytes are not zero-terminated, and a zeroed string, of length 0,\n// holds a null pointer. A string is one value that happens to occupy two\n// words, so it is one struct rather than a pointer and a length passed\n// separately, and can be returned, stored and nested like any other.\ntypedef struct {\n    const unsigned char *ptr;\n    int64_t len;\n} tack_string;\n\n// TACK_C_STR_LEN(a) is the length of the string the byte array a holds,\n// its size less the zero that ends it. A pointer does not compile.\n//\n// The second term is 0 for an array and fails to compile for a pointer,\n// whose size says nothing about its string: an array and a pointer to its\n// first element have different types, and a pointer and one to its first\n// element have the same, which makes the char array's size negative. The\n// generated source reads the length of each string in its pool with it.\n#define TACK_C_STR_LEN(x) (sizeof (x) - 1 + 0 * sizeof (char[1 - 2 * __builtin_types_compatible_p(__typeof__(x), __typeof__(&(x)[0]))]))\n// TACK_STR(lit) is the string literal lit as a tack_string. Only a\n// literal compiles.\n#define TACK_STR(lit) ((tack_string){(const unsigned char *)(\"\" lit \"\"), sizeof (\"\" lit \"\") - 1})\n// TACK_STR_N(p, n) is the n bytes at p, a const unsigned char * or an\n// unsigned char *, as a tack_string. Any other pointer does not compile.\n//\n// _Generic restricts p to the two pointers a string's bytes can be, so a\n// char * or NULL fails to compile rather than converting silently, as it\n// would under the build's -w.\n#define TACK_STR_N(p, n) ((tack_string){_Generic((p), const unsigned char *: (p), unsigned char *: (p)), (int64_t)(n)})\n\n// A Tack error, passed by value: a code, 0 when there is no error, and a\n// message, whose bytes must outlive every use of the error, as a string\n// literal's do. Only the code says whether there is an error; a zeroed\n// tack_error, code 0 and a message of length 0 holding a null pointer, is\n// no error, Tack's noerror. A function that returns an error returns it\n// as its last result, in its results struct when it has several.\ntypedef struct {\n    int32_t code;\n    tack_string msg;\n} tack_error;\n\n// TACK_ERROR(code, lit) is an error with that code and the string literal\n// lit as its message: TACK_ERROR(2, \"not found\"). Only a literal compiles,\n// as for TACK_STR.\n#define TACK_ERROR(code, lit) ((tack_error){(int32_t)(code), TACK_STR(lit)})\n// TACK_NO_ERROR is no error, as noerror is in Tack: code 0 and an empty\n// message.\n#define TACK_NO_ERROR ((tack_error){0, {0, 0}})\n\n// A Tack any, passed by value: the id of the type of the value it refers\n// to, one of the TACK_TYPE_ ids below, and a pointer to that value, which\n// is valid only for the call it was passed to. C reads the value only\n// through the pointer, after checking the id, as the helpers below do,\n// and never writes through it, keeps it or returns it: the value may be a\n// constant in read-only memory, and is the caller's, gone once the call\n// returns. ...any arrives as a view of them, tack_view_any, declared in\n// the program's tack.h.\ntypedef struct {\n    uint32_t type;\n    const void *ptr;\n} tack_any;\n\n// The type ids an any can hold, one for each type a value can be passed\n// as any with, fixed for good: C is written against them. 0 is no type,\n// so a zeroed tack_any is recognisably empty; 29 to 255 are kept for\n// types to come. Each names the C type ptr points to: bool, int8_t\n// through uint64_t, size_t for unsafe_size_t, uintptr_t for\n// unsafe_uintptr_t, tack_string, and, at 25 and 26, float for float32\n// and double for float64; and a view of each of them, from 13 for the\n// first twelve, its id 12 more than its element's, and at 27 and 28 for\n// float32 and float64, ptr pointing at the view\n// itself, a struct of a const pointer to its first element, ptr, and\n// their number, len, an int64_t: tack_view_uint8 for TACK_TYPE_VIEW_UINT8,\n// with a tack_view_ name, as the program's tack.h declares it when an\n// extern signature uses that view. The elements are the caller's too,\n// and as read-only, and for as long, as the view itself. No helper below\n// unpacks a view, since this file declares no view type: C checks the id\n// with TACK_ANY_IS(a, VIEW_UINT8), then reads *(const tack_view_uint8\n// *)a.ptr.\n#define TACK_TYPE_INVALID 0\n#define TACK_TYPE_BOOL 1\n#define TACK_TYPE_INT8 2\n#define TACK_TYPE_INT16 3\n#define TACK_TYPE_INT32 4\n#define TACK_TYPE_INT64 5\n#define TACK_TYPE_UINT8 6\n#define TACK_TYPE_UINT16 7\n#define TACK_TYPE_UINT32 8\n#define TACK_TYPE_UINT64 9\n#define TACK_TYPE_UNSAFE_SIZE_T 10\n#define TACK_TYPE_UNSAFE_UINTPTR_T 11\n#define TACK_TYPE_STRING 12\n#define TACK_TYPE_VIEW_BOOL 13\n#define TACK_TYPE_VIEW_INT8 14\n#define TACK_TYPE_VIEW_INT16 15\n#define TACK_TYPE_VIEW_INT32 16\n#define TACK_TYPE_VIEW_INT64 17\n#define TACK_TYPE_VIEW_UINT8 18\n#define TACK_TYPE_VIEW_UINT16 19\n#define TACK_TYPE_VIEW_UINT32 20\n#define TACK_TYPE_VIEW_UINT64 21\n#define TACK_TYPE_VIEW_UNSAFE_SIZE_T 22\n#define TACK_TYPE_VIEW_UNSAFE_UINTPTR_T 23\n#define TACK_TYPE_VIEW_STRING 24\n#define TACK_TYPE_FLOAT32 25\n#define TACK_TYPE_FLOAT64 26\n#define TACK_TYPE_VIEW_FLOAT32 27\n#define TACK_TYPE_VIEW_FLOAT64 28\n\n// TACK_ANY_IS(a, T) reports whether the tack_any a holds a T, where T is\n// the end of one of the ids above: TACK_ANY_IS(a, INT64). Any other T\n// does not compile.\n#define TACK_ANY_IS(a, T) ((a).type == TACK_TYPE_##T)\n\n// The unpack helpers. Each, named after a type, stores the value a holds\n// in *v and returns true when a holds that type, and returns false,\n// leaving *v alone, otherwise: tack_any_int64(a, &v). They return a copy,\n// never the pointer, so C has to cast const away to write the caller's\n// value, which it must not do. They are functions rather than macros so\n// that clang checks v's type, which a cast in a macro would hide.\nstatic inline bool tack_any_bool(tack_any a, bool *v) {\n    if (a.type != TACK_TYPE_BOOL) {\n        return false;\n    }\n    *v = *(const bool *)a.ptr;\n    return true;\n}\n\nstatic inline bool tack_any_int8(tack_any a, int8_t *v) {\n    if (a.type != TACK_TYPE_INT8) {\n        return false;\n    }\n    *v = *(const int8_t *)a.ptr;\n    return true;\n}\n\nstatic inline bool tack_any_int16(tack_any a, int16_t *v) {\n    if (a.type != TACK_TYPE_INT16) {\n        return false;\n    }\n    *v = *(const int16_t *)a.ptr;\n    return true;\n}\n\nstatic inline bool tack_any_int32(tack_any a, int32_t *v) {\n    if (a.type != TACK_TYPE_INT32) {\n        return false;\n    }\n    *v = *(const int32_t *)a.ptr;\n    return true;\n}\n\nstatic inline bool tack_any_int64(tack_any a, int64_t *v) {\n    if (a.type != TACK_TYPE_INT64) {\n        return false;\n    }\n    *v = *(const int64_t *)a.ptr;\n    return true;\n}\n\nstatic inline bool tack_any_uint8(tack_any a, uint8_t *v) {\n    if (a.type != TACK_TYPE_UINT8) {\n        return false;\n    }\n    *v = *(const uint8_t *)a.ptr;\n    return true;\n}\n\nstatic inline bool tack_any_uint16(tack_any a, uint16_t *v) {\n    if (a.type != TACK_TYPE_UINT16) {\n        return false;\n    }\n    *v = *(const uint16_t *)a.ptr;\n    return true;\n}\n\nstatic inline bool tack_any_uint32(tack_any a, uint32_t *v) {\n    if (a.type != TACK_TYPE_UINT32) {\n        return false;\n    }\n    *v = *(const uint32_t *)a.ptr;\n    return true;\n}\n\nstatic inline bool tack_any_uint64(tack_any a, uint64_t *v) {\n    if (a.type != TACK_TYPE_UINT64) {\n        return false;\n    }\n    *v = *(const uint64_t *)a.ptr;\n    return true;\n}\n\nstatic inline bool tack_any_unsafe_size_t(tack_any a, size_t *v) {\n    if (a.type != TACK_TYPE_UNSAFE_SIZE_T) {\n        return false;\n    }\n    *v = *(const size_t *)a.ptr;\n    return true;\n}\n\nstatic inline bool tack_any_unsafe_uintptr_t(tack_any a, uintptr_t *v) {\n    if (a.type != TACK_TYPE_UNSAFE_UINTPTR_T) {\n        return false;\n    }\n    *v = *(const uintptr_t *)a.ptr;\n    return true;\n}\n\nstatic inline bool tack_any_float32(tack_any a, float *v) {\n    if (a.type != TACK_TYPE_FLOAT32) {\n        return false;\n    }\n    *v = *(const float *)a.ptr;\n    return true;\n}\n\nstatic inline bool tack_any_float64(tack_any a, double *v) {\n    if (a.type != TACK_TYPE_FLOAT64) {\n        return false;\n    }\n    *v = *(const double *)a.ptr;\n    return true;\n}\n\nstatic inline bool tack_any_string(tack_any a, tack_string *v) {\n    if (a.type != TACK_TYPE_STRING) {\n        return false;\n    }\n    *v = *(const tack_string *)a.ptr;\n    return true;\n}\n\n// tack_any_signed(a, &v) stores in v the value of a signed integer of any\n// width, widened to int64_t, which keeps it, and returns true, or returns\n// false, leaving v alone, when a holds anything else. It is what %d needs.\nstatic inline bool tack_any_signed(tack_any a, int64_t *v) {\n    switch (a.type) {\n    case TACK_TYPE_INT8:\n        *v = *(const int8_t *)a.ptr;\n        return true;\n    case TACK_TYPE_INT16:\n        *v = *(const int16_t *)a.ptr;\n        return true;\n    case TACK_TYPE_INT32:\n        *v = *(const int32_t *)a.ptr;\n        return true;\n    case TACK_TYPE_INT64:\n        *v = *(const int64_t *)a.ptr;\n        return true;\n    default:\n        return false;\n    }\n}\n\n// tack_any_unsigned(a, &v) is tack_any_signed for the unsigned integers,\n// unsafe_size_t and unsafe_uintptr_t among them, widened to uint64_t,\n// which holds each at any width a target gives it.\nstatic inline bool tack_any_unsigned(tack_any a, uint64_t *v) {\n    switch (a.type) {\n    case TACK_TYPE_UINT8:\n        *v = *(const uint8_t *)a.ptr;\n        return true;\n    case TACK_TYPE_UINT16:\n        *v = *(const uint16_t *)a.ptr;\n        return true;\n    case TACK_TYPE_UINT32:\n        *v = *(const uint32_t *)a.ptr;\n        return true;\n    case TACK_TYPE_UINT64:\n        *v = *(const uint64_t *)a.ptr;\n        return true;\n    case TACK_TYPE_UNSAFE_SIZE_T:\n        *v = *(const size_t *)a.ptr;\n        return true;\n    case TACK_TYPE_UNSAFE_UINTPTR_T:\n        *v = *(const uintptr_t *)a.ptr;\n        return true;\n    default:\n        return false;\n    }\n}\n\n// A Tack Allocator, passed by value: an opaque handle on one of the\n// allocators std's C keeps, the index of its entry and the generation of\n// that entry, so a handle to an allocator that has ended can be told from\n// one to its successor: a 16-bit index, then a 48-bit generation, its\n// high 16 bits and its low 32. c_allocator is index 0, generation 0, and\n// lowers to C's malloc, realloc and free; each open region is an entry\n// of its own, its generation one that no region opened before it took\n// (see tack_region_push_). A list holds the handle of the allocator it\n// grows and is freed through. C never makes one, changes one or reads its\n// fields.\ntypedef struct {\n    uint16_t index;\n    uint16_t gen_high;\n    uint32_t gen_low;\n} tack_allocator;\n\n// TACK_C_ALLOCATOR is c_allocator.\n#define TACK_C_ALLOCATOR ((tack_allocator){0, 0, 0})\n\n// A Tack list, list[T], is a struct of its own per element type, which\n// the program's tack.h defines, tack_list_int64 for list[int64]:\n//\n//     typedef struct {\n//         T *ptr;               // the buffer, null while it is empty\n//         int64_t len;          // the number of elements\n//         int64_t cap;          // how many the buffer has room for\n//         tack_allocator alloc; // what it grows and is freed through\n//     } tack_list_T;\n//\n// The list belongs to Tack: C gets one only as a mut parameter, a pointer\n// to the caller's list, which it may read, write elements of and append\n// to during the call through the macros below, and never keeps, frees,\n// shrinks, or gives another buffer or allocator.\n\n// tack_list_grow_ returns a buffer with room for at least need elements of\n// size elem and alignment align, holding the cap elements of ptr's, from\n// the allocator a, and stores its capacity in *newcap; ptr may be null when\n// cap is 0. ptr itself is returned when it has room already. It returns\n// null, leaving ptr and *newcap as they were, when need is negative, the\n// size of the buffer would not fit in a size_t or a ptrdiff_t, the\n// alignment is more than the allocator gives, a is no allocator, or the\n// allocator fails. TACK_LIST_APPEND and TACK_LIST_RESERVE call it, and\n// TACK_LIST_APPEND_N through TACK_LIST_RESERVE; nothing else should.\nvoid *tack_list_grow_(void *ptr, int64_t cap, int64_t need, size_t elem, size_t align, tack_allocator a,\n                      int64_t *newcap);\n\n// tack_list_free_ gives a list's buffer, ptr, of cap elements of size elem\n// and alignment align, back to the allocator a it came from. A null ptr,\n// an empty list's, is nothing to free. The generated source calls it when\n// a list's owner's block ends; C must not.\nvoid tack_list_free_(void *ptr, int64_t cap, size_t elem, size_t align, tack_allocator a);\n\n// tack_allocators_release_all_ makes every allocator free every buffer it\n// has given out and not had back, allocating nothing. exit calls it just\n// before it ends the process, as do tack_stdio_failed_, before the end a\n// broken pipe makes, and the abort of an unreachable, so no buffer\n// outlives the program even though none of them runs defers; nothing\n// else may, since every list still holding one of those buffers is left\n// pointing at freed memory.\nvoid tack_allocators_release_all_(void);\n\n// TACK_UNREACHABLE(at) ends the process at an unreachable statement, at\n// being where it is written as a string literal, file:line: it writes\n// \"tack: unreachable reached at <at> (compiler bug)\" and a newline to\n// stderr and aborts, through tack_unreachable_. The checker proves no run\n// reaches such a statement, so reaching one is a bug in that proof. The\n// generated source writes it in place of each unreachable; nothing else\n// may.\n#define TACK_UNREACHABLE(at) \\\n    tack_unreachable_(\"tack: unreachable reached at \" at \" (compiler bug)\\n\")\n\n// tack_unreachable_ writes msg to stderr, makes every allocator free\n// every buffer it still holds, as exit does, and aborts. Only\n// TACK_UNREACHABLE calls it.\n_Noreturn void tack_unreachable_(const char *msg);\n\n// tack_start_ ignores SIGPIPE, so a write to a pipe with no reader fails\n// with EPIPE rather than kill the process with nothing freed. The\n// generated C's main calls it first, in a program that runs any C beyond\n// its own: one that calls print or exit, or declares an extern func,\n// std's included; nothing else may.\nvoid tack_start_(void);\n\n// tack_end_ flushes stdout, ending the process as tack_stdio_failed_\n// does when the flush fails with EPIPE. The generated C's main calls it\n// once Tack's main has returned, wherever it calls tack_start_; nothing\n// else may.\nvoid tack_end_(void);\n\n// tack_stdio_failed_ is called right after a write or a flush of C's\n// stdout or stderr fails, with errno as the failure left it; the caller\n// sets errno to 0 just before the call, so a failure that sets no errno,\n// as fwrite on a wide-oriented stream does, is not taken for an earlier\n// call's EPIPE. When errno\n// is EPIPE, the stream's pipe has no reader, and it ends the process as\n// exit(141) does: every allocator frees every buffer it still holds, no\n// defer runs, and the status is 141, 128 plus SIGPIPE's 13. Otherwise it\n// returns, and the bytes are lost. print, exit and std/fmt's C call it;\n// a write to any other descriptor must not, since its EPIPE is an error\n// for the program to see.\nvoid tack_stdio_failed_(void);\n\n// tack_ending_ marks the process as ending with status, so that\n// tack_stdio_failed_, reached again from a destructor or atexit handler\n// while C's exit runs, returns rather than call exit inside exit, and\n// records status for tack_exit_status_. exit calls it; nothing else may.\nvoid tack_ending_(int32_t status);\n\n// tack_exit_status_ reports whether exit, or tack_stdio_failed_ finding\n// a broken pipe, has begun to end the process, and stores the status it\n// ends with in *status: the code exit was called with, or 141 for the\n// broken pipe, whichever began the end. Neither has when Tack's main\n// returns, or C code calls C's exit itself. It allocates nothing, so an\n// atexit handler may call it; std/testing's does, to tell a test that\n// called exit from one whose output pipe broke.\nbool tack_exit_status_(int32_t *status);\n\n// tack_c_allocator_live_ returns how many buffers c_allocator has given\n// out and not had back, for tests that check every list is freed.\nint64_t tack_c_allocator_live_(void);\n\n// tack_c_allocator_fail_after_ makes c_allocator grant the next n requests\n// for memory and refuse every one after them, or refuse none again when\n// n is negative, for tests of what a failed allocation does.\nvoid tack_c_allocator_fail_after_(int64_t n);\n\n// tack_region_push_ opens a region, the innermost one, and returns its\n// allocator, which hands out memory from chunks it takes from\n// c_allocator, giving each back only when the region is popped. Opening\n// one allocates nothing, but the region table, which holds an entry for\n// each open region, may grow, through c_allocator; when it cannot, when\n// the region would be 65534 or more deep, so its 16-bit index would not\n// fit, or once every 48-bit generation a region can take has been taken,\n// the region opens with the dead handle, which no entry ever matches, so\n// every allocation through it fails. It never fails otherwise. The generated\n// source calls it where a region's block starts; C must not.\ntack_allocator tack_region_push_(void);\n\n// tack_region_pop_ ends a, the innermost open region, which\n// tack_region_push_ gave: it gives back every chunk the region took, and\n// the region table once no region is open, so a handle a still held\n// matches no entry. For the dead handle it only counts the region ended.\n// The generated source calls it as a region's block is left, after the\n// block's defers and frees; C must not.\nvoid tack_region_pop_(tack_allocator a);\n\n// tack_region_generation_near_max_ makes the regions opened after it\n// take generations counting up from 2^48 - 1 - left, so left of them\n// open before one would take 2^48 - 1, the dead handle's generation,\n// and that one and every one after it open with the dead handle, for\n// tests of what reaching the last generation does without opening 2^48\n// regions.\nvoid tack_region_generation_near_max_(uint64_t left);\n\n// tack_region_limit_ makes regions opened n or more deep, counting from\n// 0, open with the dead handle, or only those 65534 or more deep again\n// when n is negative or more than that, for tests of what reaching the\n// deepest index does without opening 65534 regions.\nvoid tack_region_limit_(int64_t n);\n\n// tack_argc_ and tack_argv_ are the argc and argv C's main was given,\n// which the generated main stores before it calls Tack's main, and which\n// nothing else writes: std/os reads its arguments from them. The\n// generated source defines them, so every program has them.\nextern int tack_argc_;\nextern char **tack_argv_;\n\n// TACK_LIST_LEN(l) is the number of elements of the list l, a\n// tack_list_ struct, not a pointer to one.\n#define TACK_LIST_LEN(l) ((l).len)\n\n// TACK_LIST_AT(l, i) is element i of the list l, an lvalue, which C reads\n// and writes as any other. Nothing checks i: C must keep it at least 0\n// and below TACK_LIST_LEN(l), as it must every index it uses.\n#define TACK_LIST_AT(l, i) ((l).ptr[i])\n\n// TACK_LIST_APPEND(lp, v) appends v to the list lp points to, growing its\n// buffer through its allocator when it is full, and is true; or, when the\n// buffer cannot grow, false, the list left as it was. lp and v are each\n// evaluated once, lp first, and v is converted to the element type as an\n// assignment would, so clang checks it as one, and before the buffer can\n// move, so v may read an element of the list. v is the macro's variadic\n// argument, so a compound literal, whose braces hold commas, can be one:\n// TACK_LIST_APPEND(l, (Point){1, 2}).\n#define TACK_LIST_APPEND(lp, ...) __extension__ ({ \\\n    __typeof__(lp) tack_lp_ = (lp); \\\n    __typeof__(*tack_lp_->ptr) tack_v_ = (__VA_ARGS__); \\\n    bool tack_ok_ = true; \\\n    if (tack_lp_->len == tack_lp_->cap) { \\\n        int64_t tack_cap_ = tack_lp_->cap; \\\n        int64_t tack_need_ = tack_lp_->len < INT64_MAX ? tack_lp_->len + 1 : -1; \\\n        void *tack_p_ = tack_list_grow_(tack_lp_->ptr, tack_lp_->cap, tack_need_, \\\n            sizeof *tack_lp_->ptr, _Alignof(__typeof__(*tack_lp_->ptr)), tack_lp_->alloc, &tack_cap_); \\\n        if (tack_p_ == 0) { \\\n            tack_ok_ = false; \\\n        } else { \\\n            tack_lp_->ptr = tack_p_; \\\n            tack_lp_->cap = tack_cap_; \\\n        } \\\n    } \\\n    if (tack_ok_) { \\\n        tack_lp_->ptr[tack_lp_->len++] = tack_v_; \\\n    } \\\n    tack_ok_; \\\n})\n\n// TACK_LIST_RESERVE(lp, n) makes room in the list lp points to for n\n// more elements, an int64_t, growing its buffer through its allocator\n// when it has less, so that its capacity is at least its length plus n,\n// and is true; or, when n is negative, its length plus n overflows an\n// int64_t, or the buffer cannot grow, false, the list left as it was. Its\n// length and elements never change. The buffer grows as an append's\n// does, to at least double its capacity, so making room for one element\n// before each append keeps appending in amortized constant time. lp and n\n// are each evaluated once, lp first.\n#define TACK_LIST_RESERVE(lp, n) __extension__ ({ \\\n    __typeof__(lp) tack_rlp_ = (lp); \\\n    int64_t tack_rn_ = (n); \\\n    bool tack_rok_ = tack_rn_ >= 0 && tack_rn_ <= INT64_MAX - tack_rlp_->len; \\\n    if (tack_rok_ && tack_rlp_->len + tack_rn_ > tack_rlp_->cap) { \\\n        int64_t tack_rcap_ = tack_rlp_->cap; \\\n        void *tack_rp_ = tack_list_grow_(tack_rlp_->ptr, tack_rlp_->cap, tack_rlp_->len + tack_rn_, \\\n            sizeof *tack_rlp_->ptr, _Alignof(__typeof__(*tack_rlp_->ptr)), tack_rlp_->alloc, &tack_rcap_); \\\n        if (tack_rp_ == 0) { \\\n            tack_rok_ = false; \\\n        } else { \\\n            tack_rlp_->ptr = tack_rp_; \\\n            tack_rlp_->cap = tack_rcap_; \\\n        } \\\n    } \\\n    tack_rok_; \\\n})\n\n// TACK_LIST_APPEND_N(lp, src, n) appends the n elements src points to, an\n// int64_t count of a pointer to the list's element type, to the list lp\n// points to, all or none: it makes room for all of them first, as\n// TACK_LIST_RESERVE(lp, n) does, and is true; or, when it cannot, false,\n// the list left as it was, its length, capacity and elements. n of 0\n// appends nothing, allocates nothing and is true, and src may then be\n// null. lp, src and n are each evaluated once, in that order. src must\n// not point into the list's own buffer, which making room may move.\n#define TACK_LIST_APPEND_N(lp, src, n) __extension__ ({ \\\n    __typeof__(lp) tack_alp_ = (lp); \\\n    const __typeof__(*tack_alp_->ptr) *tack_asrc_ = (src); \\\n    int64_t tack_an_ = (n); \\\n    bool tack_aok_ = TACK_LIST_RESERVE(tack_alp_, tack_an_); \\\n    if (tack_aok_) { \\\n        for (int64_t tack_ai_ = 0; tack_ai_ < tack_an_; tack_ai_++) { \\\n            tack_alp_->ptr[tack_alp_->len + tack_ai_] = tack_asrc_[tack_ai_]; \\\n        } \\\n        tack_alp_->len += tack_an_; \\\n    } \\\n    tack_aok_; \\\n})\n\n// TACK_LIST_POP(lp) is the last element of the list lp points to, which\n// it takes out of the list, leaving it one element shorter. The list\n// must not be empty: C must check TACK_LIST_LEN first, as Tack proves it\n// or checks it. lp is evaluated once. Nothing is copied, allocated or\n// freed: an element that holds a list is its taker's from then on, and\n// no longer the list's to free.\n#define TACK_LIST_POP(lp) __extension__ ({ \\\n    __typeof__(lp) tack_plp_ = (lp); \\\n    tack_plp_->ptr[--tack_plp_->len]; \\\n})\n\n// TACK_LIST_REMOVE(lp, i) is element i of the list lp points to, an\n// int64_t, which it takes out of the list, moving every element after it\n// down one place, so the rest keep their order and the list is one\n// element shorter. i must be at least 0 and below the list's length: C\n// must check it first, as Tack proves it or checks it. lp and i are each\n// evaluated once, lp first. Like TACK_LIST_POP, it allocates and frees\n// nothing, and the element taken is its taker's from then on.\n#define TACK_LIST_REMOVE(lp, i) __extension__ ({ \\\n    __typeof__(lp) tack_xlp_ = (lp); \\\n    int64_t tack_xi_ = (i); \\\n    __typeof__(*tack_xlp_->ptr) tack_xv_ = tack_xlp_->ptr[tack_xi_]; \\\n    for (int64_t tack_xj_ = tack_xi_ + 1; tack_xj_ < tack_xlp_->len; tack_xj_++) { \\\n        tack_xlp_->ptr[tack_xj_ - 1] = tack_xlp_->ptr[tack_xj_]; \\\n    } \\\n    tack_xlp_->len--; \\\n    tack_xv_; \\\n})\n\n// A Tack arena, arena[T], is a struct of its own per element type, which\n// only the generated source defines and reads: a list of every element\n// but the sentinel, and the sentinel, held inline. An id, id[T], is a\n// uint32_t, and a span, span[T], a uint64_t, its start in the low 32 bits\n// and its length in the high 32. No arena, id or span crosses to C.\n\n// tack_arena_added_ is what the generated source's add gives: the new\n// element's id and an error, as a function's results struct holds them.\ntypedef struct {\n    uint32_t r0;\n    tack_error r1;\n} tack_arena_added_;\n\n// tack_arena_spanned_ is what the generated source's add_all gives: the\n// span of the new elements and an error.\ntypedef struct {\n    uint64_t r0;\n    tack_error r1;\n} tack_arena_spanned_;\n\n// tack_arena_room_ reports whether an arena of len elements, its\n// sentinel among them, can take n more without holding more than\n// 4294967295, 2^32 - 1, or the lower limit tack_arena_limit_ set, so\n// every id and span start fits a uint32_t. It is false for a negative len\n// or n. The generated source asks it before every add, add_all and\n// reserve; C must not.\nbool tack_arena_room_(int64_t len, int64_t n);\n\n// tack_arena_limit_ makes the most elements an arena may hold n, its\n// sentinel among them, or 2^32 - 1 again when n is negative or more than\n// that, for tests of what reaching the limit does without allocating\n// 2^32 elements.\nvoid tack_arena_limit_(int64_t n);\n\n// A Tack pool, pool[T], is a struct of its own per element type, which\n// only the generated source defines and reads: a buffer of slots, each an\n// element, its generation and whether it is live, the number of live\n// elements, the first free slot and the pool's tag. A handle, handle[T],\n// is a tack_handle_ whatever T is. No pool or handle crosses to C.\n\n// tack_handle_ names an element of a pool: its slot's index, the slot's\n// generation when the element was inserted, and the tag of the pool that\n// gave it. A lookup finds the element only while all three match, so a\n// handle whose element was removed, or that another pool gave, finds\n// none. The zeroed handle has tag 0, which no pool has, so it finds none.\ntypedef struct {\n    uint32_t index;\n    uint32_t gen;\n    uint32_t tag;\n} tack_handle_;\n\n// tack_handle_eq_ reports whether a and b are one handle: the same index,\n// generation and tag. The generated source compares handles with it.\nstatic inline bool tack_handle_eq_(tack_handle_ a, tack_handle_ b) {\n    return a.index == b.index && a.gen == b.gen && a.tag == b.tag;\n}\n\n// tack_pool_inserted_ is what the generated source's insert gives: the\n// new element's handle and an error, as a function's results struct\n// holds them.\ntypedef struct {\n    tack_handle_ r0;\n    tack_error r1;\n} tack_pool_inserted_;\n\n// tack_pool_tag_ returns the tag of a pool being made: the next of a\n// counter that starts at 1 and stops at 4294967295, 2^32 - 1, which every\n// pool made after it is reached shares, so it never returns 0 and never\n// fails. The generated source calls it in every make of a pool; C must\n// not.\nuint32_t tack_pool_tag_(void);\n\n// tack_pool_next_tag_ makes n the tag the next pool made takes, for tests\n// of what reaching the counter's last value does without making 2^32\n// pools; n is clamped to 1 to 4294967295.\nvoid tack_pool_next_tag_(int64_t n);\n\n// tack_pool_gen0_ returns the generation a pool's slot starts at when the\n// pool grows to make it: 0, or what tack_pool_first_gen_ set. The\n// generated source calls it for every slot it adds; C must not.\nuint32_t tack_pool_gen0_(void);\n\n// tack_pool_first_gen_ makes n the generation every slot a pool grows\n// from then on starts at, for tests of what a generation's last value\n// does without 2^32 removals; n is clamped to 0 to 4294967295.\nvoid tack_pool_first_gen_(int64_t n);\n\n// tack_pool_room_ reports whether a pool of len slots can grow one more\n// without holding more than 4294967295, 2^32 - 1, or the lower limit\n// tack_pool_limit_ set, so every slot's index fits a uint32_t. It is false\n// for a negative len. The generated source asks it before an insert grows\n// a pool; C must not.\nbool tack_pool_room_(int64_t len);\n\n// tack_pool_limit_ makes the most slots a pool may hold n, or 2^32 - 1\n// again when n is negative or more than that, for tests of what reaching\n// the limit does without allocating 2^32 slots.\nvoid tack_pool_limit_(int64_t n);\n\n#endif\n"

// runtime_names are the base names of the files of the group runtime.
pub var runtime_names = [5]string{
    "exit.c",
    "list.c",
    "print.c",
    "process.c",
    "unreachable.c",
}

// runtime_data are the bytes of the files runtime_names names, in the same
// order.
pub var runtime_data = [5]string{
    "#include <errno.h>\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\n#include <tack/tack.h>\n\n// exit: end the process, running no defers. stdout is flushed first, here\n// rather than by C's exit, which would not report its failure: a flush\n// that finds stdout's pipe with no reader ends the process through\n// tack_stdio_failed_, with status 141 rather than code; errno is cleared\n// before it, as before every checked write. Exit then marks the process\n// as ending with code (tack_ending_), so a destructor that writes during\n// C's exit and finds the broken pipe cannot call exit again, and an\n// atexit handler can read the status (tack_exit_status_). Every allocator\n// then frees every buffer it still holds, so nothing the program\n// allocated outlives it, whatever still held it. The status is an\n// int32_t, which C's exit takes as an int with no narrowing; only its low\n// bits reach the OS. It never returns, which _Noreturn tells C, as the\n// generated C's prototype does: a function with a result may end in a\n// call to it.\n_Noreturn void tack_builtin_exit(int32_t code) {\n    errno = 0;\n    if (fflush(stdout) == EOF) {\n        tack_stdio_failed_();\n    }\n    tack_ending_(code);\n    tack_allocators_release_all_();\n    exit(code);\n}\n",
    "#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\n#include <tack/tack.h>\n\n// The C of lists: the allocators a list grows and is freed through, and\n// the functions std's tack.h declares for them, the limit on an arena's\n// length, whose elements are a list's, and a pool's tags, the generation\n// its slots start at and the limit on its slots, which a list's buffer\n// holds. A list's buffer is only\n// ever obtained, grown and given back here, so every unchecked step of a\n// list's memory is in this file.\n//\n// An Allocator is a handle on an entry of allocators, the index of the\n// entry and its generation, or on an open region. allocators has one\n// entry, c_allocator's, index 0, which lowers to malloc, realloc and\n// free and counts the buffers it has given out and not had back, so\n// tests can check that every list was freed. A region's handle is the\n// index of its entry in the region table, plus 1, and its generation\n// (see Regions, below). A handle whose index or generation is no\n// entry's is no allocator: growing through one fails, and freeing\n// through one does nothing, since the memory, if any, is not this\n// allocator's to free.\n//\n// Every allocator keeps track of every buffer it has given out and not\n// had back, and can free them all at once: exit, the end a broken pipe\n// makes (tack_stdio_failed_) and the abort of an unreachable call\n// tack_allocators_release_all_ before they end the process, so nothing a\n// program allocated outlives it, whatever still held it. A region's\n// chunks, and the region table, are c_allocator's buffers, so c_allocator\n// frees them then, and they have no other owner to free them twice.\n\n// An allocator: its generation, which a handle must match, how it grows a\n// buffer and frees one, and how it frees every buffer it still holds.\n// grow returns a buffer of bytes bytes holding the first old bytes of\n// ptr, which it may move, or null, leaving ptr as it was. release_all\n// frees every buffer grow has given out and free has not had back, and\n// must allocate nothing, since it runs when an allocator may be refusing\n// every request.\ntypedef struct {\n    uint64_t gen;\n    size_t align;\n    void *(*grow)(void *ptr, size_t old, size_t bytes);\n    void (*free)(void *ptr, size_t bytes);\n    void (*release_all)(void);\n} tack_alloc_entry_;\n\n// A c_header sits in front of every buffer c_allocator gives out, which\n// starts just past it, and links the buffer into c_held, the list of every\n// buffer c_allocator holds. It is a union with max_align_t so its size is\n// a multiple of max_align_t's alignment: malloc's memory is aligned for\n// max_align_t, so the buffer past the header is too, as it would be\n// without one.\ntypedef union c_header {\n    struct {\n        union c_header *prev;\n        union c_header *next;\n    } link;\n    max_align_t align_;\n} c_header;\n\n// c_held is the head of the circular, doubly linked list of the headers\n// of every buffer c_allocator holds; it links to itself while there are\n// none.\nstatic c_header c_held = {.link = {&c_held, &c_held}};\n\n// How many buffers c_allocator has given out and not had back, and how\n// many more requests for memory it grants, or -1 for every one.\nstatic int64_t c_live = 0;\nstatic int64_t c_grants = -1;\n\n// c_link puts h at the front of c_held.\nstatic void c_link(c_header *h) {\n    h->link.prev = &c_held;\n    h->link.next = c_held.link.next;\n    c_held.link.next->link.prev = h;\n    c_held.link.next = h;\n}\n\n// c_unlink takes h out of c_held.\nstatic void c_unlink(c_header *h) {\n    h->link.prev->link.next = h->link.next;\n    h->link.next->link.prev = h->link.prev;\n}\n\n// c_grow is c_allocator's grow: malloc for a buffer it has not given out,\n// realloc for one it has, each with room for the header in front.\nstatic void *c_grow(void *ptr, size_t old, size_t bytes) {\n    (void)old;\n    if (c_grants == 0) {\n        return 0;\n    }\n    if (c_grants > 0) {\n        c_grants--;\n    }\n    // fits keeps bytes within PTRDIFF_MAX, but the header must fit on top.\n    uint64_t limit = PTRDIFF_MAX < SIZE_MAX ? (uint64_t)PTRDIFF_MAX : (uint64_t)SIZE_MAX;\n    if (bytes > limit - sizeof(c_header)) {\n        return 0;\n    }\n    if (ptr == 0) {\n        c_header *h = malloc(sizeof(c_header) + bytes);\n        if (h == 0) {\n            return 0;\n        }\n        c_link(h);\n        c_live++;\n        return h + 1;\n    }\n    // realloc copies the links with the header, so after a move the\n    // neighbors, which still point at the old header, are pointed at the\n    // new one; nothing reads the old header after realloc. On failure\n    // the old buffer, still linked, is left as it was.\n    c_header *h = realloc((c_header *)ptr - 1, sizeof(c_header) + bytes);\n    if (h == 0) {\n        return 0;\n    }\n    h->link.prev->link.next = h;\n    h->link.next->link.prev = h;\n    return h + 1;\n}\n\n// c_free is c_allocator's free.\nstatic void c_free(void *ptr, size_t bytes) {\n    (void)bytes;\n    c_header *h = (c_header *)ptr - 1;\n    c_unlink(h);\n    free(h);\n    c_live--;\n}\n\n// c_release_all is c_allocator's release_all: it frees every buffer in\n// c_held and leaves c_held empty. Nothing frees one of those buffers\n// again: only exit calls it, and exit returns to no list that held one.\nstatic void c_release_all(void) {\n    c_header *h = c_held.link.next;\n    while (h != &c_held) {\n        c_header *next = h->link.next;\n        free(h);\n        c_live--;\n        h = next;\n    }\n    c_held.link.prev = &c_held;\n    c_held.link.next = &c_held;\n}\n\n// The allocators, by index. c_allocator's buffers are aligned for any\n// type that has a fundamental alignment, max_align_t's: malloc's are,\n// and the header in front of each is a multiple of that alignment.\nstatic const tack_alloc_entry_ allocators[] = {\n    {0, _Alignof(max_align_t), c_grow, c_free, c_release_all},\n};\n\n// handle_gen returns the generation a holds, 48 bits in two parts.\nstatic uint64_t handle_gen(tack_allocator a) {\n    return (uint64_t)a.gen_high << 32 | a.gen_low;\n}\n\n// entry returns a's allocator, or null when a is no allocator's handle.\nstatic const tack_alloc_entry_ *entry(tack_allocator a) {\n    if (a.index >= sizeof allocators / sizeof allocators[0] || allocators[a.index].gen != handle_gen(a)) {\n        return 0;\n    }\n    return &allocators[a.index];\n}\n\n// fits returns the size in bytes of n elements of size elem in *bytes,\n// and whether it is a size an allocation can have: it fits a size_t and\n// a ptrdiff_t, so a pointer can move across all of it. A size of 0 is\n// asked for as 1, since a buffer must be a pointer that is not null.\nstatic bool fits(int64_t n, size_t elem, size_t *bytes) {\n    if (n < 0) {\n        return false;\n    }\n    if (elem == 0) {\n        *bytes = 1;\n        return true;\n    }\n    uint64_t un = (uint64_t)n;\n    uint64_t limit = PTRDIFF_MAX < SIZE_MAX ? (uint64_t)PTRDIFF_MAX : (uint64_t)SIZE_MAX;\n    if (un > limit / elem) {\n        return false;\n    }\n    *bytes = (size_t)(un * elem);\n    if (*bytes == 0) {\n        *bytes = 1;\n    }\n    return true;\n}\n\n// The regions. Regions nest strictly, since each is a block, so the open\n// ones are a stack, the region table, indexed by depth: the region opened\n// at depth d, counting from 0, is entry d, and its handle has index d + 1,\n// in 16 bits. Each entry holds its region's generation, which a handle\n// must match, whether it is open, and the chunks it took from\n// c_allocator, newest first. Every region opened takes a generation of\n// its own, the next of one 48-bit counter, so no handle of a region that\n// ended matches the entry its depth reuses. A handle is 8 bytes: the\n// index, then the generation's high 16 bits and its low 32.\n//\n// Both saturate. A region opens with the dead handle, index\n// REGION_DEAD_INDEX and generation REGION_DEAD_GEN, which no entry ever\n// holds, when its depth is REGION_SLOTS or more, so its index would not\n// fit below REGION_DEAD_INDEX, when the table cannot grow to hold its\n// entry, or once the counter has reached REGION_DEAD_GEN - 1, where it\n// stays, so no generation is ever taken twice: every allocation through\n// the dead handle fails, and its pop only counts it ended. The table is a c_allocator buffer, made\n// when the first region opens and freed when the last one ends, so it is\n// owned by exactly one end: the last pop, or release_all when the process\n// ends with regions open.\n//\n// A region hands out memory by bumping through its newest chunk, each\n// buffer aligned for max_align_t, as c_allocator's are. It remembers the\n// last buffer it handed out, so growing that one extends it in place when\n// the chunk has room, or, when it is the chunk's only buffer, grows the\n// chunk itself, so a list appended to in a loop does not copy each time;\n// any other buffer grows into a fresh one, the old one's bytes copied.\n// Freeing the last buffer hands its bytes out again; freeing any other\n// does nothing, its bytes held until the region ends.\n\n// REGION_DEAD_INDEX and REGION_DEAD_GEN are the dead handle's: the\n// largest 16-bit index and the largest 48-bit generation.\n#define REGION_DEAD_INDEX UINT16_MAX\n#define REGION_DEAD_GEN ((UINT64_C(1) << 48) - 1)\n\n// REGION_SLOTS is the most entries the region table may hold: every\n// entry's index, its depth plus 1, stays below REGION_DEAD_INDEX.\n#define REGION_SLOTS (UINT16_MAX - 1u)\n\n// REGION_FIRST_SLOTS is how many entries the table has room for when the\n// first region opens; it doubles from there.\n#define REGION_FIRST_SLOTS 16u\n\n// REGION_CHUNK is the size of the room a region's chunk has when the\n// buffer it is taken for needs less.\n#define REGION_CHUNK 65536u\n\n// A region_chunk sits in front of the room of a chunk a region took from\n// c_allocator, which starts just past it: the chunk taken before it, or\n// null, and the room's size. Like c_header it is a union with max_align_t,\n// so the room is aligned for max_align_t.\ntypedef union region_chunk {\n    struct {\n        union region_chunk *prev;\n        size_t size;\n    } c;\n    max_align_t align_;\n} region_chunk;\n\n// A region_entry is an entry of the region table: the generation its\n// region took, whether that region is open, its newest chunk, or null\n// before its first allocation, how many bytes of that chunk's room are\n// handed out, and where in it the last buffer handed out starts, when\n// last is set.\ntypedef struct {\n    uint64_t gen;\n    bool open;\n    bool haslast;\n    region_chunk *chunk;\n    size_t used;\n    size_t last;\n} region_entry;\n\n// The region table, null while no region is open, how many entries it has\n// room for, how many regions are open, dead ones included, and the\n// generation the last region opened took.\nstatic region_entry *regions = 0;\nstatic uint32_t region_cap = 0;\nstatic uint64_t region_depth = 0;\nstatic uint64_t region_gen = 0;\n\n// region_slots is the depth from which regions open dead: REGION_SLOTS,\n// or less while a test has lowered it (see tack_region_limit_).\nstatic uint32_t region_slots = REGION_SLOTS;\n\n// region_handle returns the handle of the region at index, of\n// generation gen.\nstatic tack_allocator region_handle(uint16_t index, uint64_t gen) {\n    return (tack_allocator){index, (uint16_t)(gen >> 32), (uint32_t)gen};\n}\n\n// region_room returns the first byte of c's room.\nstatic char *region_room(region_chunk *c) {\n    return (char *)(c + 1);\n}\n\n// region_of returns the open region a is the handle of, or null when it\n// is none's: c_allocator's, the dead handle, or a region's that ended.\nstatic region_entry *region_of(tack_allocator a) {\n    if (a.index == 0 || a.index == REGION_DEAD_INDEX || (uint32_t)a.index - 1 >= region_cap) {\n        return 0;\n    }\n    region_entry *e = &regions[a.index - 1];\n    if (!e->open || e->gen != handle_gen(a)) {\n        return 0;\n    }\n    return e;\n}\n\n// region_take returns a buffer of bytes bytes from e's newest chunk, or\n// from a new one it takes from c_allocator, with room for at least\n// REGION_CHUNK bytes, when the newest has too little room; or null,\n// leaving e as it was, when c_allocator cannot give one.\nstatic void *region_take(region_entry *e, size_t bytes) {\n    size_t align = _Alignof(max_align_t);\n    if (e->chunk != 0) {\n        size_t size = e->chunk->c.size;\n        size_t start = e->used + (align - e->used % align) % align;\n        if (start >= e->used && start <= size && bytes <= size - start) {\n            e->last = start;\n            e->haslast = true;\n            e->used = start + bytes;\n            return region_room(e->chunk) + start;\n        }\n    }\n    size_t size = bytes > REGION_CHUNK ? bytes : REGION_CHUNK;\n    if (size > SIZE_MAX - sizeof(region_chunk)) {\n        return 0;\n    }\n    region_chunk *c = c_grow(0, 0, sizeof(region_chunk) + size);\n    if (c == 0) {\n        return 0;\n    }\n    c->c.prev = e->chunk;\n    c->c.size = size;\n    e->chunk = c;\n    e->last = 0;\n    e->haslast = true;\n    e->used = bytes;\n    return region_room(c);\n}\n\n// region_grow is a region's grow, as an allocator's is: ptr, when not\n// null, is a buffer of old bytes e handed out, and the result holds its\n// bytes. The last buffer handed out grows in place when its chunk has\n// room, or, as its chunk's only buffer, by growing the chunk; any other\n// grows into a fresh buffer.\nstatic void *region_grow(region_entry *e, void *ptr, size_t old, size_t bytes) {\n    if (ptr != 0 && e->haslast && (char *)ptr == region_room(e->chunk) + e->last) {\n        if (bytes <= e->chunk->c.size - e->last) {\n            e->used = e->last + bytes;\n            return ptr;\n        }\n        if (e->last == 0 && bytes <= SIZE_MAX - sizeof(region_chunk)) {\n            region_chunk *c = c_grow(e->chunk, sizeof(region_chunk) + e->chunk->c.size, sizeof(region_chunk) + bytes);\n            if (c == 0) {\n                return 0;\n            }\n            c->c.size = bytes;\n            e->chunk = c;\n            e->used = bytes;\n            return region_room(c);\n        }\n    }\n    void *p = region_take(e, bytes);\n    if (p != 0 && ptr != 0) {\n        memcpy(p, ptr, old < bytes ? old : bytes);\n    }\n    return p;\n}\n\n// region_free is a region's free: it hands the bytes of ptr out again\n// when it is the last buffer e handed out, and does nothing otherwise.\nstatic void region_free(region_entry *e, void *ptr) {\n    if (e->haslast && (char *)ptr == region_room(e->chunk) + e->last) {\n        e->used = e->last;\n        e->haslast = false;\n    }\n}\n\ntack_allocator tack_region_push_(void) {\n    const tack_allocator dead = region_handle(REGION_DEAD_INDEX, REGION_DEAD_GEN);\n    uint64_t depth = region_depth++;\n    if (region_gen >= REGION_DEAD_GEN - 1 || depth >= region_slots) {\n        return dead;\n    }\n    if (depth >= region_cap) {\n        uint32_t cap = region_cap == 0 ? REGION_FIRST_SLOTS : region_cap;\n        if (region_cap != 0) {\n            cap = region_cap > REGION_SLOTS / 2 ? REGION_SLOTS : region_cap * 2;\n        }\n        if ((uint64_t)cap > (uint64_t)(SIZE_MAX / sizeof(region_entry))) {\n            return dead;\n        }\n        region_entry *grown = c_grow(regions, (size_t)region_cap * sizeof(region_entry), (size_t)cap * sizeof(region_entry));\n        if (grown == 0) {\n            return dead;\n        }\n        for (uint32_t i = region_cap; i < cap; i++) {\n            grown[i] = (region_entry){0};\n        }\n        regions = grown;\n        region_cap = cap;\n    }\n    region_entry *e = &regions[depth];\n    e->gen = ++region_gen;\n    e->open = true;\n    e->haslast = false;\n    e->chunk = 0;\n    e->used = 0;\n    e->last = 0;\n    return region_handle((uint16_t)(depth + 1), e->gen);\n}\n\nvoid tack_region_pop_(tack_allocator a) {\n    region_depth--;\n    region_entry *e = region_of(a);\n    if (e != 0) {\n        region_chunk *c = e->chunk;\n        while (c != 0) {\n            region_chunk *prev = c->c.prev;\n            c_free(c, sizeof(region_chunk) + c->c.size);\n            c = prev;\n        }\n        *e = (region_entry){0};\n    }\n    if (region_depth == 0 && regions != 0) {\n        c_free(regions, (size_t)region_cap * sizeof(region_entry));\n        regions = 0;\n        region_cap = 0;\n    }\n}\n\nvoid tack_region_generation_near_max_(uint64_t left) {\n    region_gen = left >= REGION_DEAD_GEN ? 0 : REGION_DEAD_GEN - 1 - left;\n}\n\nvoid tack_region_limit_(int64_t n) {\n    region_slots = n < 0 || n > (int64_t)REGION_SLOTS ? REGION_SLOTS : (uint32_t)n;\n}\n\nvoid *tack_list_grow_(void *ptr, int64_t cap, int64_t need, size_t elem, size_t align, tack_allocator a,\n                      int64_t *newcap) {\n    const tack_alloc_entry_ *al = entry(a);\n    region_entry *re = al == 0 ? region_of(a) : 0;\n    size_t most = al != 0 ? al->align : _Alignof(max_align_t);\n    if ((al == 0 && re == 0) || need < 0 || cap < 0 || align > most) {\n        return 0;\n    }\n    if (need <= cap && ptr != 0) {\n        return ptr;\n    }\n    // Double the capacity, from 4, so n appends move the buffer about\n    // log2(n) times, but never past what fits; then at least need.\n    int64_t want = cap < 4 ? 4 : cap;\n    if (want <= INT64_MAX / 2 && cap >= 4) {\n        want = cap * 2;\n    }\n    if (want < need) {\n        want = need;\n    }\n    size_t bytes, old = 0;\n    if (!fits(want, elem, &bytes)) {\n        want = need;\n        if (!fits(want, elem, &bytes)) {\n            return 0;\n        }\n    }\n    if (ptr != 0 && !fits(cap, elem, &old)) {\n        return 0;\n    }\n    void *p = al != 0 ? al->grow(ptr, old, bytes) : region_grow(re, ptr, old, bytes);\n    if (p == 0) {\n        return 0;\n    }\n    *newcap = want;\n    return p;\n}\n\nvoid tack_list_free_(void *ptr, int64_t cap, size_t elem, size_t align, tack_allocator a) {\n    (void)align;\n    const tack_alloc_entry_ *al = entry(a);\n    size_t bytes = 0;\n    if (ptr == 0) {\n        return;\n    }\n    if (al == 0) {\n        region_entry *re = region_of(a);\n        if (re != 0) {\n            region_free(re, ptr);\n        }\n        return;\n    }\n    if (!fits(cap, elem, &bytes)) {\n        bytes = 0;\n    }\n    al->free(ptr, bytes);\n}\n\nvoid tack_allocators_release_all_(void) {\n    for (size_t i = 0; i < sizeof allocators / sizeof allocators[0]; i++) {\n        allocators[i].release_all();\n    }\n}\n\nint64_t tack_c_allocator_live_(void) {\n    return c_live;\n}\n\nvoid tack_c_allocator_fail_after_(int64_t n) {\n    c_grants = n < 0 ? -1 : n;\n}\n\n// arenaMax is the most elements an arena may hold, its sentinel among\n// them: 2^32 - 1, so every id, below it, fits a uint32_t, or less while a\n// test has lowered it (see tack_arena_limit_).\n#define ARENA_MAX 4294967295u\nstatic uint64_t arenaMax = ARENA_MAX;\n\nbool tack_arena_room_(int64_t len, int64_t n) {\n    if (len < 0 || n < 0 || (uint64_t)len > arenaMax) {\n        return false;\n    }\n    return (uint64_t)n <= arenaMax - (uint64_t)len;\n}\n\nvoid tack_arena_limit_(int64_t n) {\n    arenaMax = n < 0 || (uint64_t)n > ARENA_MAX ? ARENA_MAX : (uint64_t)n;\n}\n\n// poolTag is the tag the next pool made takes: it starts at 1, so no pool\n// has tag 0, the zero handle's, and stops at POOL_MAX, which every pool\n// made after it is reached shares, so making a pool never fails (see\n// tack_pool_next_tag_).\n#define POOL_MAX 4294967295u\nstatic uint32_t poolTag = 1;\n\nuint32_t tack_pool_tag_(void) {\n    uint32_t tag = poolTag;\n    if (poolTag < POOL_MAX) {\n        poolTag++;\n    }\n    return tag;\n}\n\nvoid tack_pool_next_tag_(int64_t n) {\n    poolTag = n < 1 ? 1 : (uint64_t)n > POOL_MAX ? POOL_MAX : (uint32_t)n;\n}\n\n// poolGen0 is the generation a pool's new slot starts at: 0, or more while\n// a test has raised it (see tack_pool_first_gen_).\nstatic uint32_t poolGen0 = 0;\n\nuint32_t tack_pool_gen0_(void) {\n    return poolGen0;\n}\n\nvoid tack_pool_first_gen_(int64_t n) {\n    poolGen0 = n < 0 ? 0 : (uint64_t)n > POOL_MAX ? POOL_MAX : (uint32_t)n;\n}\n\n// poolMax is the most slots a pool may hold: 2^32 - 1, so every index,\n// below it, fits a uint32_t, or less while a test has lowered it (see\n// tack_pool_limit_).\nstatic uint64_t poolMax = POOL_MAX;\n\nbool tack_pool_room_(int64_t len) {\n    return len >= 0 && (uint64_t)len < poolMax;\n}\n\nvoid tack_pool_limit_(int64_t n) {\n    poolMax = n < 0 || (uint64_t)n > POOL_MAX ? POOL_MAX : (uint64_t)n;\n}\n",
    "#include <errno.h>\n#include <stdio.h>\n\n#include <tack/tack.h>\n\n// print: write s to stdout exactly as given. An empty string is not\n// written: a zeroed one holds a null pointer, which C does not let fwrite\n// take even with nothing to write. A write that fails is handed to\n// tack_stdio_failed_, which ends the process when stdout's pipe has no\n// reader, and otherwise loses the bytes. errno is cleared first, so a\n// write that fails without setting it, as fwrite on a wide-oriented\n// stream does, is not taken for an earlier call's EPIPE.\nvoid tack_builtin_print(tack_string s) {\n    if (s.len <= 0) {\n        return;\n    }\n    errno = 0;\n    if (fwrite(s.ptr, 1, (size_t)s.len, stdout) < (size_t)s.len) {\n        tack_stdio_failed_();\n    }\n}\n",
    "// process: the start of the process, its end after Tack's main returns,\n// and the end a broken pipe on standard output or standard error makes.\n//\n// A write to a pipe or socket with no reader raises SIGPIPE, whose\n// default action kills the process at once, freeing nothing. So the\n// generated C's main calls tack_start_ first, which ignores SIGPIPE, and\n// such a write fails with EPIPE instead. A write to standard output or\n// standard error that fails with EPIPE then ends the process as\n// exit(141) does: every allocator frees every buffer it still holds, no\n// defer runs, and the status is 141, 128 plus SIGPIPE's number, 13, the\n// status a shell reports for a process SIGPIPE killed. print, exit and\n// std/fmt's C call tack_stdio_failed_ after every write and flush of\n// either stream that fails, which ends the process so when the failure\n// is EPIPE. A write to any other descriptor, std/os's, gets EPIPE as an\n// error it returns.\n//\n// C's stdout is buffered, so a broken pipe may only be found when the\n// buffer is flushed: by a later write, by exit, which flushes stdout\n// itself before C's exit would, or, when Tack's main returns, by\n// tack_end_, which the generated main calls then. Either way the status\n// is 141, whatever exit was asked for.\n//\n// An ignored signal stays ignored across exec, so std/os's run sets\n// SIGPIPE back to its default action in every child it starts: a child\n// behaves as it would started from a shell.\n//\n// Either end records its status, which tack_exit_status_ returns, so an\n// atexit handler, std/testing's, can tell what ended the process.\n#include <errno.h>\n#include <signal.h>\n#include <stdbool.h>\n#include <stdint.h>\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\n#include <tack/tack.h>\n\n// broken_pipe_status is the status a broken pipe ends the process with.\nenum { broken_pipe_status = 141 };\n\n// ending is whether the process is ending already, through exit or\n// through tack_stdio_failed_. C's exit runs the C library's own flush of\n// stdout, which finds a broken pipe again and reports it to no one, and\n// any destructor an extern C file has; should one of those reach\n// tack_stdio_failed_, it returns, losing the bytes, rather than call exit\n// inside exit.\nstatic bool ending = false;\n\n// exit_status is the status the process is ending with, once exit was\n// called or a broken pipe found, which exit_known says (see\n// tack_exit_status_).\nstatic int32_t exit_status = 0;\nstatic bool exit_known = false;\n\n// tack_start_ ignores SIGPIPE. signal fails only for a signal number that\n// is not one, or one that cannot be ignored, and SIGPIPE is neither, so\n// its result is not checked.\nvoid tack_start_(void) {\n    (void)signal(SIGPIPE, SIG_IGN);\n}\n\n// tack_end_ flushes stdout once Tack's main has returned, so a broken\n// pipe the flush finds ends the process with status 141, where C's exit\n// would flush it and return 0.\nvoid tack_end_(void) {\n    errno = 0;\n    if (fflush(stdout) == EOF) {\n        tack_stdio_failed_();\n    }\n}\n\n// tack_ending_ marks the process as ending with status, for exit.\nvoid tack_ending_(int32_t status) {\n    ending = true;\n    exit_status = status;\n    exit_known = true;\n}\n\n// tack_exit_status_ stores the status the process is ending with in\n// *status and reports whether exit or a broken pipe has begun to end it.\nbool tack_exit_status_(int32_t *status) {\n    *status = exit_status;\n    return exit_known;\n}\n\n// tack_stdio_failed_ ends the process as exit(141) does when errno, which\n// the caller cleared before the failed write or flush of stdout or\n// stderr, is EPIPE, and returns otherwise, the bytes lost.\nvoid tack_stdio_failed_(void) {\n    if (errno != EPIPE || ending) {\n        return;\n    }\n    ending = true;\n    exit_status = broken_pipe_status;\n    exit_known = true;\n    tack_allocators_release_all_();\n    exit(broken_pipe_status);\n}\n",
    "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\n#include <tack/tack.h>\n\n// unreachable: the abort of an unreachable statement the checker proved\n// no run reaches, so reaching one is a compiler bug. It writes msg, which\n// TACK_UNREACHABLE builds and which already ends in a newline, to stderr,\n// frees every allocator's buffers, as exit does, so nothing the program\n// allocated outlives it, and aborts, running no defers. It never returns,\n// which _Noreturn tells C: a function with a result may end in it.\n//\n// The fputs is not checked, nor handed to tack_stdio_failed_: this is the\n// abort of a compiler bug, which ends the process with abort whatever the\n// write did, a broken pipe included.\n_Noreturn void tack_unreachable_(const char *msg) {\n    fputs(msg, stderr);\n    tack_allocators_release_all_();\n    abort();\n}\n",
}
